// Structure to store semaphore information
struct semaphore {
    int value; // Current value of the semaphore
    pthread_mutex_t mutex; // Mutex for synchronizing access to the semaphore
    pthread_cond_t cond; // Condition variable for signaling changes in the semaphore value
};

// Initialize a new semaphore with a specified initial value
int init_semaphore(struct semaphore *sem, int value) {
    if (sem == NULL || value < 0) {
        return -1; // Invalid input
    }

    // Initialize the mutex and condition variable
    pthread_mutex_init(&sem->mutex, NULL);
    pthread_cond_init(&sem->cond, NULL);

    // Set the initial value of the semaphore
    sem->value = value;

    return 0; // Success
}

// Wait for the semaphore to become available
int wait_semaphore(struct semaphore *sem) {
    if (sem == NULL) {
        return -1; // Invalid input
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&sem->mutex);

    // Wait for the condition variable to be signaled or until the timeout expires
    while (sem->value <= 0 && !pthread_cond_wait(&sem->cond, &sem->mutex)) {
        // Do nothing - wait indefinitely
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&sem->mutex);

    return 0; // Success
}

// Signal the semaphore to become available
int signal_semaphore(struct semaphore *sem) {
    if (sem == NULL) {
        return -1; // Invalid input
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&sem->mutex);

    // Increment the value of the semaphore
    sem->value++;

    // Signal the condition variable to wake up any waiting threads
    pthread_cond_signal(&sem->cond);

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&sem->mutex);

    return 0; // Success
}

// Delete the semaphore
int delete_semaphore(struct semaphore *sem) {
    if (sem == NULL) {
        return -1; // Invalid input
    }

    pthread_mutex_lock(&sem->mutex);

    // Wait for any active threads to finish using the semaphore
    while (sem->value > 0) {
        // Do nothing - wait indefinitely
    }

    // Destroy the mutex and condition variable
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&sem->mutex);
    pthread_cond_destroy(&sem->cond);

    return 0; // Success
}
```
The `struct semaphore` structure stores information about the semaphore, including its current value and the mutex and condition variable used for synchronization. The `init_semaphore`, `wait_semaphore`, `signal_semaphore`, and `delete_semaphore` functions provide functionality for initializing, waiting on, signaling, and deleting the semaphore.

The `init_semaphore` function takes a pointer to a `struct semaphore` as input and initializes it with the specified initial value. It also initializes the mutex and condition variable used for synchronization. If the input is invalid (e.g., NULL pointer), it returns an error code.

The `wait_semaphore` function takes a pointer to a `struct semaphore` as input and waits for the semaphore to become available. It first acquires the mutex associated with the semaphore, then checks if the value of the semaphore is positive (i.e., there are active threads using it). If so, it waits indefinitely on the condition variable until the semaphore value becomes negative or a signal is received. It then releases the mutex and returns an error code if any occurred during the wait.

The `signal_semaphore` function takes a pointer to a `struct semaphore` as input and signals the semaphore to become available. It first acquires the mutex associated with the semaphore, then increments the value of the semaphore by one. It then releases the mutex and returns an error code if any occurred during the signaling operation.

The `delete_semaphore` function takes a pointer to a `struct semaphore` as input and deletes the semaphore. It first acquires the mutex associated with the semaphore, then waits for any active threads to finish using the semaphore before destroying the mutex and condition variable. If the input is invalid (e.g., NULL pointer), it returns an error code.

This implementation ensures that the semaphore is properly managed in a multi-threaded environment, preventing premature deletion and ensuring thread safety. It also allows for varying load conditions and concurrent access patterns from different threads without data races or other concurrency issues."]

