The main function first checks if the command line argument specifies the dataset size. If not, it prints an error message and exits. Otherwise, it initializes the shared arrays using the `init_arrays()` function, which allocates memory for each array and creates mutexes for each element in the arrays.

The program then defines three thread functions: `update_record()`, `update_key()`, and `update_offset()`. Each of these functions takes a single argument that specifies the index of the array element to be updated. The function acquires the lock for the corresponding element in the shared arrays, updates its value, and releases the lock.

The program then creates three threads using the `pthread_create()` function, each of which calls one of the thread functions defined above. It passes an argument (the index of the array element to be updated) to each thread so that it can specify the correct array element to update. The main function also joins all three threads using the `pthread_join()` function to ensure that they have completed their tasks before exiting.

The program finally prints the values in the shared arrays to demonstrate that the updates made by the threads are visible when the program is executed. It frees the memory allocated by the `init_arrays()` and `free_arrays()` functions using the `free()` function, and then exits.']

