For the JavaScript DOM traversal function, you can use a recursive approach to traverse the DOM tree and return a list of elements that match a given selector. Here's an example implementation:
```javascript
function dom_traverse(selector) {
    let result = [];
    // Use querySelectorAll() to find all elements matching the selector
    const elements = document.querySelectorAll(selector);
    for (let i = 0; i < elements.length; i++) {
        result.push(elements[i]);
        // If the current element has children, recursively call dom_traverse() on them
        if (elements[i].children.length > 0) {
            result = result.concat(dom_traverse(selector));
        }
    }
    return result;
}
```
