Here is a JavaScript function that recursively traverses a DOM tree and returns a list of elements that match a given selector, using a provided function to test the element's class or ID. Ensure your implementation is efficient and modular, considering potential performance issues with deeply nested elements. Optimize the JavaScript DOM traversal function by adding support for early termination if certain conditions are met (e.g., reaching a specific depth limit) to avoid unnecessary processing of deeply nested elements.
```javascript
// Function to recursively traverse a DOM tree and return a list of elements that match a given selector
function findElements(selector, element, depthLimit = 5) {
    // Create an empty array to store the matched elements
    const matches = [];

    // Check if the current element matches the selector
    if (matchesSelector(element, selector)) {
        // Add the current element to the list of matches
        matches.push(element);
    }

    // Recursively traverse the child elements
    for (let i = 0; i < element.children.length && depthLimit > 0; i++) {
        // Increase the depth limit by one
        depthLimit--;

        // Call the function recursively with the current child element and the remaining depth limit
        matches.push(...findElements(selector, element.children[i], depthLimit));
    }

    return matches;
}

// Function to test if an element matches a given selector
function matchesSelector(element, selector) {
    // Check if the element's class or ID match the selector
    if (selector === '.className') {
        return element.classList.contains(selector.substr(1));
    } else if (selector === '#idName') {
        return element.id === selector.substr(1);
    }

    // Check if the element's tag name matches the selector
    return element.tagName.toLowerCase() === selector;
}
