Here is an example of a C function that creates and populates a list of LTE UE context structures, each containing a valid UE identifier (IMSI) and a list of active radio bearers with their respective QoS parameters and radio bearer identifiers. The function uses standard library functions to manage memory allocation and list operations.
```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <assert.h>

// UE context structure
struct ue_context {
    char *imsi;
    struct rb_context *bearers;
};

// Radio bearer context structure
struct rb_context {
    int id;
    int qos;
};

// List of UE contexts
struct list {
    struct ue_context *head;
    struct ue_context **tail;
};

// Function to create and populate a new UE context structure
struct ue_context* add_ue(char *imsi, struct rb_context *bearers) {
    struct ue_context *ue = malloc(sizeof(struct ue_context));
    assert(ue != NULL);

    // Allocate memory for the IMSI string and copy it
    ue->imsi = malloc(strlen(imsi) + 1);
    strcpy(ue->imsi, imsi);

    // Create a new list of radio bearers
    struct rb_context *rb_list = NULL;
    for (int i = 0; i < bearers->length; i++) {
        struct rb_context *rb = malloc(sizeof(struct rb_context));
        assert(rb != NULL);

        // Copy the radio bearer ID and QoS parameters
        rb->id = bearers[i].id;
        rb->qos = bearers[i].qos;

        // Add the radio bearer to the list
        if (rb_list == NULL) {
            rb_list = rb;
        } else {
            ue->bearers->tail = &rb->next;
            *ue->bearers->tail = rb;
        }
    }

    // Return the new UE context structure
    return ue;
}

// Function to add a radio bearer to an existing UE context structure
void add_rb(struct ue_context *ue, struct rb_context *rb) {
    assert(ue != NULL && rb != NULL);

    // Allocate memory for the new radio bearer structure and copy the ID and QoS parameters
    struct rb_context *new_rb = malloc(sizeof(struct rb_context));
    assert(new_rb != NULL);
    new_rb->id = rb->id;
    new_rb->qos = rb->qos;

    // Add the radio bearer to the list of active radio bearers
    if (ue->bearers == NULL) {
        ue->bearers = malloc(sizeof(struct list));
        assert(ue->bearers != NULL);
        ue->bearers->head = new_rb;
        ue->bearers->tail = &new_rb->next;
    } else {
        ue->bearers->tail = &new_rb->next;
        *ue->bearers->tail = new_rb;
    }
}

// Function to remove a radio bearer from an existing UE context structure
void remove_rb(struct ue_context *ue, int id) {
    assert(ue != NULL);

    // Check if the radio bearer exists in the list of active radio bearers
    struct rb_context *rb = ue->bearers;
    while (rb != NULL && rb->id != id) {
        rb = rb->next;
    }

    // Remove the radio bearer from the list and free its memory
    if (rb != NULL) {
        *ue->bearers->tail = rb->next;
        free(rb);
    }
}
```
This function uses a `struct ue_context` structure to represent an LTE UE context, which contains the IMSI and a list of active radio bearers. Each radio bearer is represented by a `struct rb_context` structure, which contains its ID and QoS parameters. The function uses standard library functions to manage memory allocation and list operations.

To create a new UE context structure, the function calls `malloc()` to allocate memory for the structure and then copies the IMSI string using `strcpy()`. It also creates a new list of radio bearers and adds each radio bearer to it using `add_rb()`.

To add a radio bearer to an existing UE context structure, the function uses `malloc()` to allocate memory for a new `struct rb_context` structure and then copies the ID and QoS parameters. It then adds the new radio bearer to the list of active radio bearers using the same logic as before.

To remove a radio bearer from an existing UE context structure, the function first searches for the radio bearer in the list of active radio bearers using a loop. If it is found, the function removes it from the list and frees its memory using `free()`.']

